\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
The concept of Wave-Particle Duality holds that light simultaneously exibits both the properties of a wave of energy and a particle of matter.  While this might not hold much significance to the average person, it was one of the single events that helped 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
o shake the foundation of physics to the ground.  It was the gateway to the beginning of what has become the field of science known as Quantum Mechanics.  With the introduction of Einstein's Theory of Relativity, classical physics was blown to pieces.  Es
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 s\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
encialy, the theory says "what would the observations look like if the observer was moving at or near the speed of light?"  Since this had been the reference point or the "foundation" of physics definitions over the years, changing this concept had incred
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 i\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
bly large ramifications.  It is what led to the discovery of the dual nature of the particle.  Once the observer is moving at or near the speed of light, light no longer exists as a wave of energy, but as a photon, a particle.  The Double Slit Experiment 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 s\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 hows how light acts as both a wave and a particle by equating it to waves seen on the surface of water.
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs20\insrsid3932697 \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 The Double Slit Experiment
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid3932697 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\fs20\insrsid3932697 \loch\af0\dbch\af31505\hich\f0 \'93\loch\f0 Shoot electrons (or anything for that matter) at a screen with two slits and record their position of impact at a detector
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
 behind the screen.  You will observe an interference pattern just like the one produced by diffraction of a light or water wave at two slits.  This pattern will even appear if you slow down the electron source so that only one electron per second comes t
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 h\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
rough.  Classically speaking, every electron either travels through the first or the second slit.  This means that we should be able to produce the same interference pattern if we ran the experiment twice as long, closing slit number one for the first hal
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 f\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 , then closing slit number two for the second half.  This does not apply, as the pattern will not emerge.  This pattern is a result of the electron\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s wave function being diffracted by both slits and interfering with itself.  Until an observation is made, \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 \hich\f0 
he position of a particle is described in terms of probability waves, but after the particle is observed, it is described as a fixed value.\'94
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin720\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\fs20\insrsid3932697 
